# Git normalises text files to CRLF on checkout on Windows, which
# rewrites this file inside the built bundle and makes every Shorebird
# patch look like an asset change — 69 carriage returns in a README no
# code reads, enough to stop a patch that is otherwise Dart-only.
# The shipped releases carry it with LF, so pin it there.
assets/**/README.md text eol=lf

# Same reason, different blast radius: GitHub Actions extracts a `run:`
# block into a shell script on a Linux runner. A stray CR makes every
# line die as `$'\r': command not found`, so the watcher would fail
# daily on its own formatting rather than on anything it watches.
.github/workflows/*.yml text eol=lf

# The same class of bug again, and the one where it would be fatal: a
# font checked out with CRLF normalisation applied is a corrupt font.
# Git detects these as binary on its own; saying so is cheap insurance
# on a repo where a rewritten asset has already cost a release — and
# the incident that cost it was an image, so the pictures are named
# here too: 400-odd scene posters and every bundled scenery.
*.ttf binary
*.mp3 binary
*.mp4 binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.png binary

# And the two shapes the card composer's library ships in. Gzip is
# binary — Git works that out for itself, and saying so is the same
# cheap insurance as the line above it — while the index beside it is
# JSON the app reads byte for byte, so it is pinned to LF like the
# READMEs at the top of this file: a checkout that rewrote it would
# change the bundle and stop an otherwise Dart-only patch.
*.gz binary
assets/**/*.json text eol=lf
